// BUG-ZYB-003 — a soft error has to reach a string.
//
// `>>` had always rendered one, and both the register VM and the browser
// engine concatenated one; the tree-walker alone aborted with
// `cannot juxtapose value of type Error(ErrorValue { … }) in string context`.
//
// The place it aborted is what made it serious. The ordinary way to pass a
// failure upwards is to name it in a message:
//
//     r = puede_fallar()
//     ? r$! { <~ (#0, "" r) }        // ← the tree-walker died HERE
//
// so a soft error turned into a hard one on the error-handling path — the one
// path that must not acquire a second way to fail, and the one tests exercise
// least. A program worked until the day something went wrong, and then failed
// a second, worse way.
//
// The error is built here from an out-of-range index rather than from a file or
// a database, so the message is the language's own and says the same thing on
// every platform.
riesgoso(arr, i) {
    !? {
        <~ arr[i]
    } :! {
        <~ _err
    }
}

e = riesgoso([5, 10, 15], 99)

>> "es error: " e$! ¶
>> "impreso:  " e ¶

s = "" e
>> "en cadena: " s ¶
>> "compuesto: [" e "]" ¶

// The whole point: an error can now be carried inside a value that travels.
envuelto = (#0, "" e)
>> "envuelto: " envuelto[1] " / " envuelto[2] ¶
